Description: platform: add BackNavigation, system-wide back navigation of a window
 Some environments offer one back control for all applications, e.g. the
 navigation bar of a phone shell. With the kde_back_navigation_v1 Wayland
 protocol a client tells the compositor for each xdg_toplevel whether back
 currently has an effect, and the compositor sends a back event when the
 user operates that control.
 .
 The new attached type BackNavigation lets items take part: an item sets
 enabled while going back has an effect for it and goes back one step on
 triggered(). A dispatcher per window collects them and also looks at the
 popups in the overlay of the window: the topmost open popup is closed as
 the Escape key would close it (dialogs are rejected), unless an enabled
 item inside of it handles back first; a modal popup that does not close
 on Escape blocks back navigation in the window behind it; tool tips and
 non-modal popups that stay open are ignored. Outside of popups an item
 inside another enabled item comes first, otherwise the one enabled last.
 .
 On Wayland the state is sent to the compositor through
 kde_back_navigation_v1 when it offers the global. The object is created
 for the xdg_toplevel of the window and recreated whenever Qt creates a
 new one (after hiding and showing the window). Without the global, on
 other platforms and for windows without xdg_toplevel nothing changes.
 .
 Wayland support is enabled by default on Linux and BSD (USE_WAYLAND) and
 needs Qt6WaylandClient, libwayland and wayland-protocols.
Author: pocket-debs
Forwarded: no
License: LGPL-2.0-or-later (new and patched source files); kde-back-navigation-v1.xml: MIT
Last-Update: 2026-11-27
